Author: frank <hellmann@pik-potsdam.de>
Date:   Sun Nov 22 21:23:47 2026 +0100

    runtime: a positive answer through an entry records the entries of both sides, both bounds of each variable
    
    Inference folds a positive answer `S <: X` through a membership entry only where it can record the
    entries the answer rests on (`record_memberships!`, `jl_membership_entries_for`), and the code's worlds
    start at theirs (compiler §3.1, §4.8). The walk that collects them took the open sets of X, upper bounds
    only, and the entries on S's TypeNames. A lower bound turns the question around: in
    `Tuple{SZ, Ref{LY}} <: Tuple{LZ, Ref{T}} where T >: LX` subtyping asks `LX <: LY`, the member in X's
    lower bound and the set in S. That entry was not recorded: the code folded `true` with the worlds of
    `SZ in LZ` alone, ran in a world before `LX in LY` and kept its answer when that membership was taken
    back. Now the sets are those of S and X and the entries those on the TypeNames of S and X, each
    variable's lower and upper bound included (`open_sets_in`, `membership_entries_visit`). The set is a
    superset of the entries the walk can use: a fold rests on no fewer than it uses.
    
    Reference: compiler §3.1 (a positive membership is recorded as its entry), §4.8. Retires C-161.
    Test: experiments/implementation/w143e/motiv/lb_entries.jl; gates f7_membership_edges, m1_*, m2_*,
    n2_*; test/subtype.jl.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

